To automatically generate a **Table of Contents (TOC)** in LaTeX, you need to use the `\tableofcontents` command, which automatically collects all sectioning commands (`\section`, `\subsection`, etc.) you've used in your document. Below is a complete example demonstrating how to set this up:

```latex
\documentclass{article}
\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}

\title{Example Document with Automatic TOC}
\author{Your Name}
\date{\today}

\begin{document}

% Generate the Table of Contents
\tableofcontents

\newpage % Start content on a new page

\section{Introduction}
This is the introduction section. It explains the purpose of the document.

\section{Background}
This section provides background information.

\subsection{History}
Subsection about the history of the topic.

\subsection{Key Concepts}
Subsection explaining key concepts.

\section{Methodology}
This section describes the research methods used.

\section{Results}
Here we present the results of the study.

\section{Conclusion}
The conclusion summarizes the findings and suggests future work.

\end{document}
```

### How It Works:
1. **`\tableofcontents`**: This command inserts an automatically generated table of contents at that point in the document. LaTeX tracks sectioning commands (`\section`, `\subsection`, etc.) and lists them with their page numbers.
2. **Sectioning Commands**: Use `\section{...}`, `\subsection{...}`, and `\subsubsection{...}` to structure your document. These will appear in the TOC.
3. **Compilation**: Run LaTeX twice (or use tools like `pdflatex`) to generate the TOC—LaTeX needs an extra pass to resolve references and page numbers.

### Additional Tips:
- **Hyperlinks**: To make the TOC clickable (in PDF output), add `\usepackage{hyperref}` (usually last in the preamble). Note: This may require compiling multiple times.
- **Depth**: By default, LaTeX includes up to `\subsection` in the TOC. To adjust depth, use `\setupToC` or `\secttoc` (for article class) or modify the `tocdepth` counter in the preamble: `\setcounter{tocdepth}{2}`.
- **Custom Entries**: Use `\addcontentsline{toc}{section}{Custom Title}` to manually add entries to the TOC (useful for unnumbered sections like a preface).

This example provides a straightforward way to generate an automatic TOC in LaTeX, which is especially useful for longer documents like reports, theses, and books.

